2.)
For Faculty get request diagramatic Architecture:

GET /api/academics/faculties/
            │
            ▼
    FacultyListView
            │
            ▼
     get_faculties()
            │
            ▼
       Faculty.objects
            │
            ▼
        Database

    The results travels back to:

        Database
        ↓
        Faculty objects
        ↓
        FacultySerializer
        ↓
        JSON
        ↓
        Frontend

This is the exact separation we've been practicing.
using selector gives us a upper hand as today might become:
Faculty.objects.all()

while tomorrow could be:

Faculty.objects
    .select_related(...)
    .prefetch_related(...)
    .filter(...)
    .order_by(...)


And eventually there could be permission, visibility, institution, search, or archival rules around academic records.

Centralizing reads gives us a clear place to evolve those queries.

But don't overinterpret this: selectors aren't magic. We don't create a selector for every one-line query just to increase file count. We use them to establish a consistent data-access boundary where it provides value.